From bcd426c5cc2b96f32c3d22c4e02167dc75d72c0c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:45:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] AD9959DDSSweeper: fix runviewer parser, clock limit and
 runtime checks

Fixes for the AD9959 DDS Sweeper driver added in pull request #126.

The runviewer parser could not run: it referenced an undefined name, used
np.float, which NumPy no longer has, assigned to field names that did not
match the dtype it declared, and passed the traces to add_trace without a
time axis. It is rewritten after the NovaTechDDS9M parser: the clock ticks
are taken from the clockline trace, the dynamic channels are read from
dds_data and converted with the scale factors that generate_code stores in
the device properties, the static channels are read from static_data, and
each DDS sub-channel gets a (times, values) trace.

clock_limit divided the reference clock frequency by the number of Pico
cycles per instruction. The firmware sets the Pico system clock to the
reference clock frequency only when the Pico provides the reference clock;
with an external reference clock the Pico system clock stays at its
power-up frequency of 125 MHz. The limit is now computed from the Pico
system clock, and the docstring and the device documentation say so.

sweep_mode other than 0 is rejected in the constructor; before, the table
was always written in the steps format and the mismatch was found by the
worker at run time. The remaining assert statements used as runtime checks
in the worker (firmware version, board, "ok" response) raise LabscriptError.
transition_to_manual waits for the STOPPED status for a fixed time (5 s)
instead of a fixed number of status queries. The tuning word to frequency
conversion in the worker uses 2**32, as the quantisation does. The error
text for a bad connection string says channels 0 to 3.

Documentation: "PrawnDO" replaced by "DDS Sweeper", the "open an issue"
link is now a link, and the clocking note has its body indented so that it
renders.

Verified with Python 3.14.6, NumPy 2.5.4 and PySide6 6.12.0: every module
of the device imports without a deprecation warning; an experiment with a
DummyPseudoclock, two DDS and two StaticDDS children compiles through
runmanager and runviewer's Shot class loads it, producing twelve traces
with one point per clock tick and the programmed frequencies, amplitudes
and phases; a static-only experiment does the same; sweep_mode=1 raises
LabscriptError at construction; clock_limit is 250 kHz, 166.7 kHz and
100 kHz for one, two and four dynamic channels with the default clock, and
stays at 166.7 kHz for two channels with an external 100 MHz reference.
The worker was not run against hardware.

